	// Kernel holds the corresponding private keys internally.
	// Used by SecurityInterceptor to validate tokens for cluster calls.
	// Sized for the real fleet, not for one node: kernel keeps one active
	// signing key per algorithm PER REPLICA (private keys live in each node's
	// own disk snapshot and are never shared), and a rotated public key is
	// retained for max_token_lifetime_seconds so the tokens it signed stay
	// verifiable. With the shipped defaults that is
	//
	//	replicas(3) x algorithms(2) x (2592000s retention / 86400s rotation + 1)
	//	  = 3 x 2 x 31 = 186 keys.
	//
	// The cap is 128 rather than 186 because this set is published as a
	// jwk.Jwks, whose keys field is capped at 128 by the shared, do-not-modify
	// sys/jwk.proto; KeyRotator.MAX_VERIFYING_KEYS enforces the same 128 at run
	// time and evicts oldest-first when the set would exceed it. The old 16 was
	// sized for a single node and could not hold even a healthy 3-replica set.
